    "durationMs": 400540,
    "finalText": "Done. The URL shortener API is complete and verified. Here's what I built:\n\n**Implementation** — NestJS 11.2.11 + TypeScript 5.8.3 with TypeORM 0.3/PostgreSQL (the idiomatic NestJS persistence layer), reading everything from `DATABASE_URL` (verified working against the provided `postgres:5432`):\n\n- `src/links/` — `Link` entity (`links` table: unique `code`, `url` text, `clicks` counter, creation ordering via bigint id), service with atomic `UPDATE ... clicks = clicks + 1`, DB-level unique-violation handling for 409s, and crypto-random 8-char generated codes that retry on collision.\n- `src/database/` — versioned SQL migration (`CreateLinksTable1735689600000`) applied by a standalone script with connection retry; `start.sh` builds, applies pending migrations, then execs the server in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`.\n- Error semantics — a global exception filter plus a custom JSON body pipeline so malformed JSON, invalid/missing url, and invalid codes all return **422** `{error}` (catching subtle cases like `https:///path`, which WHATWG URL parsing silently \"fixes\" but a strict host check must reject).\n\n**Verification** — a 47-case HTTP test matrix passed fully: exact response bodies/keys, duplicate-code 409, charset/length boundaries (4 and 20 chars), malformed/empty/array/null bodies, redirect `Location` + exactly-once click counting (including 10 concurrent requests), ascending list order with `total`, and a full server restart showing identical data with the click counter continuing (12 → 13) afterwards.\n\nI left the environment clean: port 8000 is free and the links table is empty with migrations already applied, so `sh start.sh` (documented in the README) is ready for the harness."
